</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Precision and recall results for open-ended and single-answer queries. <span id="S4.T4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Vital<math id="S4.T4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\textsc{Prec.}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Prec.</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\textsc{Prec.}}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span id="S4.T4.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Vital<math id="S4.T4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\textsc{Rec.}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Rec.</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\textsc{Rec.}}</annotation></semantics></math></span> show a clear difference between normal and adversarial responses, where <span id="S4.T4.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FActScore</span> and <span id="S4.T4.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">NuggetRecall</span> are less sensitive.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There are similar trends in precision and recall metrics (Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4 Results ‣ All Claims Are Equal, but Some Claims Are More Equal Than Others: Importance-Sensitive Factuality Evaluation of LLM Generations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
In <span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FActScore</span> results, we observe normal and missing responses are about the same, with only a small dip (about a 5-7% dip) for wrong responses. For open-ended query <span id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Vital<math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\textsc{Prec.}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Prec.</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\textsc{Prec.}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, we see a similar decrease. In contrast, we observe a large drop between normal/missing and wrong responses in <span id="S4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Vital<math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\textsc{Prec.}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Prec.</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\textsc{Prec.}}</annotation></semantics></math></span> (about 25% less) for single-answer queries. For <span id="S4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">NuggetRecall</span>, we observe all responses (normal/missing/wrong) are within 6% of one another for open-ended responses and 9% for single-answer responses, with the highest score for normal responses, and lowest for missing responses. Open-ended <span id="S4.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Vital<math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\textsc{Rec.}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Rec.</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\textsc{Rec.}}</annotation></semantics></math></span> exhibits similar trends, with an 11% difference for normal and missing responses. We see a much larger dip of 25% for single-answer queries.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Response-level metrics in Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4 Results ‣ All Claims Are Equal, but Some Claims Are More Equal Than Others: Importance-Sensitive Factuality Evaluation of LLM Generations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> are the best indicator of these errors in key information. Note that here, lower numbers are better, as they indicate a lower percent of error.
<span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Vital<math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\textsc{Prec.}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Prec.</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\textsc{Prec.}}</annotation></semantics></math></span> reports the percentage of responses with any vital subclaims wrong. We observe good detection of incorrect responses here, with 75% (open-ended) and almost 90% (single-answer) of wrong responses getting vital subclaims wrong.
<span id="S4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Vital<math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\textsc{Rec.}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Rec.</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\textsc{Rec.}}</annotation></semantics></math></span> indicates the percentage of responses where any vital nugget is unsupported. There is a small difference in <span id="S4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Vital<math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\textsc{Rec.}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Rec.</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\textsc{Rec.}}</annotation></semantics></math></span> scores for all responses (less than a 5% difference) for open-ended queries. However, <span id="S4.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Vital<math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\textsc{Rec.}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Rec.</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\textsc{Rec.}}</annotation></semantics></math></span> is a better detector of error in single-answer queries, with a 15% difference between normal responses and missing/wrong responses.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T5" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T5.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
