<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Prompts, Model Details, Compute</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The prompts used for generation of normal responses are shown in Table <a href="#A1.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Appendix A Prompts, Model Details, Compute ‣ All Claims Are Equal, but Some Claims Are More Equal Than Others: Importance-Sensitive Factuality Evaluation of LLM Generations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, and adversarial responses are shown in Table <a href="#A1.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Appendix A Prompts, Model Details, Compute ‣ All Claims Are Equal, but Some Claims Are More Equal Than Others: Importance-Sensitive Factuality Evaluation of LLM Generations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. For normal response generation, we prepend an instruction to answer in a paragraph for each of the queries, to ensure the text is in long-form. For missing and wrong response generation, we provide the LLM with the query and normal LLM response, and instruct the LLM to adversarially omit or falsify key information in the response. For both normal and adversarial response generation, we use <span id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-4o</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, with a temperature of 0.2 and 2000 max tokens.</p>
</div>
